Logic has a problem with referenceless nameoids.  If every (or, indeed, any=
)=20
sentence containing one is true, then Particular Generalization is invalid;=
 if=20
all (any) are false, then Universal Instantiation fails. So, either they ha=
ve no=20
truth values (with all the problems that entails) or they have stange truth=
=20
values (and that has problems, too).  Of, finally, we can deny that there a=
re=20
any referenceless nameoids. usually by forcing some randomly selected membe=
r  of=20
the universe to be the default referent of such critters.  This last move i=
s=20
nice in formal semantics, but isn't much help languages, since the status o=
f=20
such sentences here-and-now, is undetermined (back to those problems again)=
.  So=20
the best solution is just not to use such terms.  The rule that use of a te=
rm=20
guarantees its referent is in the domain does that pretty much.  But the ex=
treme=20
sort of contradictory terms, obvious ones as well as subtle, seem to foil e=
ven=20
this attempt, and {lo no broda} is one of these. But Lojban ontology is sai=
d to=20
embrace the impossible as well as the possible, So, there is a referent for=
 {lo=20
no broda} and, by other reasons, it is a broda, a medad and impossible.  No=
t a=20
happy result, perhaps, but one of the joys of logical languages is that it =
keeps=20
coming up with surprises.




